The `e2e-tick-109` test is a self-dogfooding pipeline verification for the Foreman (Off-by-One) solver. The issue is that the solver's pipeline needs to be exercised end-to-end: problem ingestion → prompt construction → LLM solving → output extraction. This tick validates that when a problem file is passed through the pipeline, the pi-agent wrapper correctly creates the prompt, invokes the pi CLI, and the model produces properly formatted sections.

The fix/improvement for this tick is ensuring the pipeline handles the full cycle correctly. Here's the relevant code path in the pi-agent wrapper at `/home/kara/.local/bin/pi-agent`:

```typescript
// The pi-agent wrapper reads the problem and constructs a prompt:
const lines = [
  "Solve this problem and respond in three sections:",
  "",
  `Problem: ${problem.problem_class}`,
  `Environment: ${problem.environment} | Language: ${problem.language} ${problem.version || ""}`,
  "",
  problem.description || "",
];

// Then invokes the pi CLI in print mode:
const result = execSync(
  `node /tmp/pi/packages/coding-agent/dist/cli.js --model "${model}" --print "@${promptFile}"`,
  { env, timeout: 1800000, maxBuffer: 1024 * 1024, encoding: "utf-8" }
);

// And parses the output by splitting on "## " markers:
const sections = result.split(/^## /m).filter(Boolean);
for (const s of sections) {
  const body = s.replace(/^(SOLUTION|EVIDENCE|SIGNATURES)\s*\n?/, "").trim();
  if (s.startsWith("SOLUTION")) sol = body;
  else if (s.startsWith("EVIDENCE")) ev = body;
  else if (s.startsWith("SIGNATURES")) {
    const m = body.match(/```(?:json)?\s*\n?([\s\S]*?)\n?```/) || [null, body];
    try { JSON.parse(m[1]); sig = m[1].trim(); } catch { sig = JSON.stringify({ model, result: "completed" }); }
  }
}
```

The pipeline works correctly when the model's response contains the three required sections with proper formatting. The self-dogfood nature means the Foreman project (Go-based off-by-one daemon) uses this same pipeline to solve its own issue tracking items (ticks).
